  "caveats": [
    "277,173 cells with uw_class == 'U' (underwriting class *unknown*, not *not applicable*) were held out of this class-conditioned fit. 'NA' rows are retained as their own stratum. Pass keep_unknown_uw_class=True to include them.",
    "2 cells had zero exposure and were dropped \u2014 a Poisson offset needs exposure * q_base > 0, and a cell nobody was exposed in carries no information. They held 0.0 deaths, as expected.",
    "The mix component of the A/E drift (-0.00099/yr) is LARGER than the experience component (-0.00020/yr). Most of what the crude A/E is doing is composition, not experience \u2014 read the standardised column, not the crude one.",
    "Cells are pooled across duration, so select-period mortality is absorbed into the attained-age effect. If the duration mix drifts with calendar year, part of that drift lands in the fitted improvement. Pass `--duration-bands` to control for it at ~9x the cell count (raw `duration_months` in `--group-by` would cost ~60x for a dimension whose signal is a smooth selection curve).",
    "Pearson dispersion \u03c6 = 2.25, so the quasi-Poisson covariance scaling was applied: every band below is 1.50x wider than a plain-Poisson fit would have reported. The point estimates are unchanged \u2014 scaling touches the covariance only. A \u03c6 this far above 1 means unmodelled heterogeneity, which is expected on real grouped data and is why the scaling is not optional here.",
    "The base offset is estimated from these same cells, so `overall_ae` is ~1 by construction and is not an independent check on the level. The fitted improvement is unaffected: it is a calendar contrast, and every calendar-invariant term \u2014 the offset included \u2014 cancels in the difference."
  ],
